{
  "dom_id": "step-q91439",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "If the speed of an object is measured in $\\text{cm/s},$ what are the units of its acceleration?",
  "worked_solution": "We can find the acceleration of an object by computing the slope of its speed-time graph. So, let's draw an example of a speed-time graph where the speed is measured in $\\text{cm/s}$ and the time is measured in $\\text{s}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/f97581877814a2626d820b07b4e7a2c3d637f9369b119d0e0c3896c77e15f964.png)\n\nIn this case, the slope is computed as\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{\\text{change in speed}}{\\text{change in time}},\n$$\n\nand so the units of the slope are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{units of speed}}{\\text{units of time}}=\\frac{\\text{cm/s}}{\\text{s}}=\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\text{s}}=\\frac{\\text{cm}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the units of acceleration are $\\frac{\\text{cm}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$",
